Sval: identities for isolated workspaces, safe from a locked containers pref

An isolated workspace's tabs share a cookie jar of their own (isolated
workspaces spec §4). A non-public identity is the kind Firefox uses for its
own internal jars: no container menu lists it, no tab is coloured for it and
no label names it in the address bar, which is what a workspace's jar needs,
since Sval draws the workspace itself. ContextualIdentityService.create makes
only public identities, remove refuses non-public ones and only a public
identity can be found by id, and no other API does any of the three. So
create takes an isPublic option, remove takes any identity except Firefox's
own system identities, and getPrivateIdentityFromId mirrors
getPublicIdentityFromId.

Sval locks privacy.userContext.enabled off, and an add-on with the
contextualIdentities permission still writes it at startup. The write
notifies observers although the locked value never changes, and this
service answers every notification that reads false by closing every tab
outside the default jar and resetting its identities, which would close an
isolated workspace's tabs and delete its jar. So a notification for a locked
pref is ignored: its value cannot have changed.

